{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lethargic-tigerseye-xenarthra","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nObserved in the DEPLOYED review-bot's logs (2026-08-28), once per run:\n\n```\ncurrent gate-ref snapshot unavailable; hashing the working tree\n  File \"/app/src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py\", line 254, in _hash_basis\n    sha = resolve_ref(gate_source.default_ref(root), root, fetch=False)\n  File \"/app/src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py\", line 292, in resolve_ref\nrebar._snapshot.git_fetch.SnapshotRefError: cannot resolve ref 'origin/main' to a commit in '/tmp/reviewbot-cjb9jh6c'.\n```\n\n## Proven mechanism\n\n`_hash_basis(repo_root, *, at_current_gate_ref=True)`\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py:246-260`) wraps the gate-ref resolution in a\nbare `except Exception`, logs a WARNING, and returns `_config.repo_root(repo_root)` —\nthe in-place working tree — as the hashing basis.\n\nIts sole production caller is the CLAIM-GATE freshness re-check\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py:605-613`, inside `compute_validity` for\n`kind == plan-review`), which re-hashes the attestation's SIGNED dependency paths\nagainst that basis to decide `stale-code`.\n\nSigning-time hashing (`manifest.py:398 dependency_hashes`, `attest.py:237 _rehash`)\nuses the OTHER branch (`current_code_root()`), so **no signed attestation's\n`verified_at_sha` or manifest hashes are affected**. The defect is entirely on the\nVERIFY side.\n\n### The fallback is fail-OPEN, contrary to its own stated rationale\n\nThe docstring claims the degradation is \"the conservative direction, since the\ncheckout normally contains the drift\". A runtime experiment falsifies this. With one\nsigned attestation and a genuinely drifted `origin/main`, toggling ONLY whether the\ngate ref resolves:\n\n* gate ref resolvable  -> `{'ok': False, 'verdict': 'stale-code'}`  (claim BLOCKED)\n* gate ref unresolvable -> `{'ok': True,  'verdict': 'certified'}`  (claim ALLOWED)\n\nWhenever the working tree happens to match the reviewed content while the gate ref has\nmoved (a worktree not yet fast-forwarded; a checkout pinned at the reviewed SHA), the\nsubstituted basis reports NO drift and the gate certifies a stale attestation.\n\n### Violated authority\n\n`docs/plan-review-gate.md:1566-1574`:\n\n    | Claim gate | **fail-closed** | a missing/stale plan-review attestation BLOCKS the claim |\n    ... reserve fail-closed for the claim gate.\n\nand ADR 0002 (`docs/adr/0002-code-drift-invalidation.md:46`) \"keeping the fallback\nfail-closed\". `verify.require_plan_review_for_claim = true` in this project's\n`rebar.toml`, so the gate is live.\n\n### Reachability\n\nThe `except Exception` catches far more than a missing ref: any failure of\n`resolve_ref` OR `_cache.acquire` (snapshot materialization) — disk pressure, a\ncorrupt/evicted cache entry, a lock timeout, a transient git fault — degrades the\nsame way, on the on-box MCP server (`REBAR_ROOT=/var/gerrit/site/mcp-code`) where\nclaims are actually decided. The only trace is a WARNING line.\n\nIn the review-bot's own clone (`gerrit_client.py:396 clone_change_ref` does\n`git init` + `git fetch <url> <patchset-ref>` with NO `origin` remote) the ref can\nNEVER resolve, so the fallback fires on 100% of runs. There the consumer is the\nbugfix-size gate, whose `ACCEPTED_VERDICTS = {certified, stale-code, stale-head}`\nabsorbs both outcomes — so the observed log line does not change the review-bot's\nvote. The review-bot is the messenger, not the victim.\n\n## What is NOT the defect\n\nThe fallback's EXISTENCE is intended and documented (ADR 0002 \"Shared ref-resolution\nboundary\"; the operator-approved decision on ticket 72d9-8fbd-3a9e-48a6 —\n\"source=local config or an unresolvable ref degrades to the working tree\"; and the\nexisting test `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_ref_coherence.py::test_hash_basis_resolution`\nasserts \"an unresolvable ref -> degrade to the working tree (never crash)\").\nThe fix must NOT remove it. The defect is that the substitution is invisible to the\nconsumer, so the fail-closed claim gate silently becomes fail-open.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWhen the claim gate's drift check cannot obtain the configured ATTESTED basis, it must\nnot certify. The substitution must be visible to the verdict consumer, and the claim\nmust be blocked with a reason naming the unobtainable basis. A configured\n`source=local` gate is UNCHANGED: there the working tree is the correct, documented\nbasis, not a degradation.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Keep `_hash_basis`'s public behaviour and signature exactly as today (the tested,\n   ADR-documented contract). Extract the gate-ref resolution into a sibling that also\n   reports whether it degraded, and have `_hash_basis` delegate to it — preserving the\n   ADR 0002 \"ONE shared ref-resolution boundary\" invariant.\n2. In `compute_validity`'s plan-review drift arm, consume the degradation signal: when\n   the gate is configured ATTESTED and the basis degraded, return the existing\n   `stale-code` verdict with a reason naming the unobtainable gate ref, instead of\n   comparing hashes against a substituted tree.\n3. Reuse the EXISTING `stale-code` literal deliberately: it is already in\n   `bugfix_size_gate.ACCEPTED_VERDICTS`, so the review-bot's vote is unchanged (it\n   already lands on `stale-code` for most runs today), while the claim gate blocks.\n   No new verdict literal, no change to `KNOWN_VERDICTS`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py`\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_ref_coherence.py`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] With an attested gate config, a signed attestation whose dependency drifted on the\n      gate ref, and a working tree still matching the signed content: the claim gate\n      returns `ok=False` / `verdict=stale-code` when the gate ref CANNOT be resolved\n      (today it returns `ok=True` / `certified`).\n- [ ] The denial reason names the unobtainable gate ref rather than claiming the files\n      drifted.\n- [ ] With the gate ref RESOLVABLE, every existing behaviour is unchanged: committed\n      drift in a signed dependency still invalidates; an unrelated committed change and an\n      uncommitted working-tree edit still do NOT.\n- [ ] A configured `source=local` gate is unchanged (working-tree basis on both sides; no\n      degradation reported).\n- [ ] `_hash_basis(..., at_current_gate_ref=True)` still returns the working-tree path on\n      an unresolvable ref (never raises) — `test_hash_basis_resolution` stays green.\n- [ ] No new verdict literal: `bugfix_size_gate.KNOWN_VERDICTS` is untouched and the\n      review-bot's bucket for these runs is unchanged.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default pytest suite exit zero.\n","id":"505d-b2c5-734f-47d9","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Claim gate fails OPEN when the current gate ref cannot be resolved: _hash_basis silently substitutes the working tree"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787960782529354400,"uuid":"61dc09b0-b7cd-4850-9770-17ae4cd23923"}